<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · British Isles</p><h1 class="st-title">Powys Fadog</h1><p class="st-meta">1160 CE – 1282 CE (approx.) · capital Castell Dinas Bran</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The northern half of the Welsh kingdom of Powys, ruled by Madog ap Maredudd's descendants from Castell Dinas Bran above Llangollen.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madog-ap-maredudd">Madog ap Maredudd</a>, the last king of all <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-powys">Powys</a>, died in 1160, his kingdom split. The northern part passed to his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gruffydd-maelor-i">Gruffydd Maelor</a> and then to Gruffydd's son Madog ap Gruffydd <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maelor">Maelor</a>, after whom it was named Powys Fadog, covering Maelor, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ial">Ial</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edeirnion">Edeirnion</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cynllaith">Cynllaith</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Its princes usually aligned with Gwynedd's <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-wales">Principality of Wales</a> against the English crown. Madog ap Gruffydd founded the Cistercian abbey of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valle-crucis-abbey">Valle Crucis</a> around 1201, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gruffydd-maelor-i">Gruffydd ap Madog</a> built <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/castell-dinas-bran">Castell Dinas Bran</a> around 1260.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Powys Fadog's princes backed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/llywelyn-ap-gruffudd">Llywelyn ap Gruffudd</a> in his final war, and after his death in 1282 Edward I granted the lands to English lords. Its line survived as minor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gentry">gentry</a>; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/owain-glyndwr">Owain Glyndwr</a>, who led the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-wales-under-owain-glyndwr">Principality of Wales under Owain Glyndwr</a>, descended from it.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/powys-fadog.jpg" alt="Powys Fadog" width="320" height="224"><figcaption>Image: AlexD · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Powys_Fadog.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1160 CE – 1282 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>principality</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Castell Dinas Bran</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Madog ap Gruffydd Maelor, Gruffydd ap Madog</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Welsh</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/united-kingdom">United Kingdom</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Northern Powys</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1221&amp;focus=powys-fadog" aria-label="Powys Fadog on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4609 353 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Powys Fadog lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M4929 609L4910 602M4929 609L4919 628M4929 609L4970 663"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4910 602h0M4919 628h0M4970 663h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4929 609h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4929 609h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Castell Dinas Bran. <a href="/atlas?year=1221&amp;focus=powys-fadog">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
